// In-depth battles, batch s: rocroi, shanhaiguan, guararapes, ambuila, saraighat, lund.
// Checked against the English, Portuguese, Spanish, French, Swedish and Chinese Wikipedia articles on each battle
// (Rocroi, Shanhai Pass, First and Second Guararapes, Mbwila, Saraighat, Lachit Borphukan, Ahom-Mughal conflicts, Lund,
// Antonio I of Kongo) and the works they cite (Tucker, Guthrie, Gonzalez de Leon, Ribas, Pacheco Fernandez; Wakeman, Mote,
// Hsi, Cao; Marley, Garcia, Boxer; Thornton; Sarkar, Gait, Bhuyan, Sharma), plus Toda Materia, Estrategia Militares,
// A Gazeta (interview with Celso Castro and Paulo Henrique Martinez on the Army Day), Live History India and Curious Indian.
// Britannica, EBSCO, Indian Culture and the Assam government site returned errors and were not used.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "terço": "A Spanish infantry unit of the 16th and 17th centuries: a large square block of pikemen and musketeers who protected one another. For more than a century nobody managed to break a tercio in open field. Portugal and colonial Brazil also used the name for their regiments.",
  "piqueiro": "An infantry soldier armed with the pike, a spear about 4 to 5 meters long. Pikemen formed a forest of points that shielded the musketeers from cavalry.",
  "metralha": "A load of small balls or scraps of iron put inside a cannon. When fired, it spreads out and sweeps a wide strip, like a giant shotgun. It is terrible against infantry standing still, at close range.",
  "caracol": "A cavalry tactic in which each rank rides forward, fires its pistols and wheels around to reload, instead of charging home. It was slow and made the attack lose its momentum.",
  "oito bandeiras": "The Manchu military system: the whole population was divided into groups, each under a colored banner. It brought together Manchus, Mongols and Han Chinese and gave their army a rare discipline.",
  "wic": "The Dutch West India Company, a Dutch firm with a monopoly on trade and conquest in the Atlantic. It was the company that took Brazil's sugar-producing Northeast, starting in 1630.",
  "quilombo": "A community of enslaved people who escaped and organized themselves on their own, usually in hard-to-reach places. Palmares, in present-day Alagoas, was the largest in colonial Brazil.",
  "manicongo": "The title of the king of Kongo. It comes from 'mani', lord, and 'Kongo'. The king was elected from among the members of the noble houses.",
  "imbangala": "Warrior bands of central Africa that lived off war and the capture of people for the slave trade. In Angola, they allied several times with the Portuguese.",
  "borphukan": "The Ahom title of the viceroy of the western part of the kingdom, based in Guwahati. He was also the commander of the army in that region."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  rocroi: {
    war: "Thirty Years' War, Franco-Spanish phase (1635–1659)",
    hook: "At 21, the Duke of Enghien broke the infantry that Europe had feared for more than a century.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1643, Europe had been at war for 25 years. The Thirty Years' War began in 1618 in the German lands and pulled in one country after another. France entered directly in 1635, against the Habsburgs of Spain and of the Holy Roman Empire. Its first invasion of the Spanish Netherlands came to nothing.",
        "The Spanish army of Flanders, for its part, had an enormous reputation. Its infantry, organized in [[tercios|terço]], was considered the best on the continent. Each tercio was a large block of [[pikemen|piqueiro]] and musketeers, and for more than a century nobody had managed to break one in open field.",
        { h: "The road to Rocroi" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1618", "The Thirty Years' War begins."],
          ["1635", "France declares war on Spain."],
          ["May 1642", "The Spanish beat the French at Honnecourt and open the way into northern France."],
          ["December 1642", "Cardinal Richelieu, who had run the war on the French side, dies."],
          ["14 May 1643", "King Louis XIII dies. Louis XIV, aged 4, takes the throne, and Mazarin continues the war."],
          ["17 May", "The Duke of Enghien learns of the king's death and hides the news from the army."],
          ["18 May", "The French reach the area around Rocroi, take a ridge overlooking the town and exchange fire with the Spanish."],
          ["19 May", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "With France in mourning and a child on the throne, the Spanish saw a chance. Their commander, Francisco de Melo, crossed the Ardennes with about 27,000 men (Tucker's figure) and besieged the fortress of Rocroi, defended by a garrison of 400 to 500 soldiers. The Spanish Wikipedia says the aim was to relieve the pressure on Catalonia and Franche-Comté, where Spain was also fighting."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "The Duke of Enghien, 21 years old" },
        "Louis II de Bourbon-Condé, the future 'Great Condé', was a prince of the blood and commanded the French army in Picardy. On learning that Rocroi was besieged, he went to relieve it. He hid the king's death from the army. And he made the decision that defined the day: to attack without waiting, against the advice of the older officers, because a Spanish reinforcement was on its way. Later, during the fight, he made the second decision that mattered: instead of falling back when things turned ugly, he used what had worked.",
        { h: "Francisco de Melo, Spanish commander" },
        "Captain-general of the army of Flanders. He had the most feared infantry in Europe and believed he had the advantage in numbers. When the French appeared, he dropped the siege and accepted battle. The Spanish Wikipedia notes that he was slow to commit his infantry at the start, which may have cost him a quick victory.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the French side, Marshal Jean de Gassion commanded the cavalry of the right wing beside Enghien; L'Hôpital led the left wing, with the Duke of La Ferté-Senneterre; Espenan commanded the infantry of the center; Sirot, the reserve. On the Spanish side, the Duke of Albuquerque commanded the cavalry of the left and the Count of Isenburg that of the right. The infantry of the center went to old Paul-Bernard de Fontaines, who fought to the death. Jean de Beck, with a reinforcement of 4,000 to 6,000 men (sources vary), was on his way."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The two armies were similar in size, and the sources do not agree on the numbers. The French had about 22,000 to 23,000 men (15,000 to 16,000 on foot, 7,000 mounted) and 12 to 14 cannon. The Spanish had between 22,000 and 26,000 (15,000 to 19,000 on foot, 5,000 to 9,000 mounted) and 18 to 24 cannon. The core of the Spanish side was some 8,000 Spanish veterans in front, with Italian, German and Walloon mercenaries behind.",
        { note: "The numbers for Rocroi change from book to book. The English, French and Spanish Wikipedias give different ranges, and the figure of 27,000 Spaniards is that of the army that left Flanders, before it detached garrisons and lost men along the way. We use ranges rather than a single number." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Open fields in front of Rocroi. To get there, the French passed through a defile between woods and marshes that the Spanish had left unguarded, and occupied a ridge above the town. In front of Albuquerque's wing there was a small wood with hidden musketeers (about a thousand, according to the French Wikipedia), and near the French left wing there was a marsh with a pond.",
        { h: "The small hours" },
        "The evening before, the armies had already exchanged fire. A deserter is said to have warned Enghien that Beck's reinforcement was near. That is what convinced him to attack at dawn, before Beck arrived.",
        { h: "The battle in six movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the two armies deployed the same way: infantry in the center in two lines, cavalry on the flanks, artillery in front. The Spanish tercios stood in the middle, and the French artillery in front of its infantry. Enghien and Gassion took the French right, and Albuquerque the Spanish left, directly opposite them.",
        "Second, on the French right, Enghien and Gassion attacked Albuquerque's cavalry. The first charge was punished by the musketeers in the wood. Enghien rallied the cavalry, destroyed the musketeers and, with Gassion's help, pushed the Spanish cavalry back.",
        "Third, on the French left, things went wrong. La Ferté's cavalry, which swerved around the marsh and left its flank exposed, was broken by Isenburg's cavalry, and La Ferté was captured. Isenburg took the French artillery and turned the guns against the center. Melo attacked Espenan's infantry, and only Sirot's reserve held the advance. The French left and center were in trouble, and the battle was undecided.",
        "Fourth, Enghien's decision. Instead of falling back to help the left, he left Gassion holding Albuquerque and took the rest of the cavalry behind the Spanish center. He hit Isenburg from behind and cut the Spanish veterans off from the Italians, Germans and Walloons. The Italians withdrew, and the Germans and Walloons left the field. The Spanish cavalry scattered, and the gunners fled.",
        "Fifth, the last stand. Alone, the Spanish tercios formed a large block and repelled two or three cavalry charges in a row, with muskets and pikes. Enghien's horse was killed under him. Beck arrived around 8 or 9 in the morning and, seeing the defeat, halted without joining the fight. Enghien gathered the artillery, including recaptured guns, and fired [[canister shot|metralha]] at close range. Fontaines died, and Castellví's tercio was destroyed.",
        "Sixth, the end. Enghien offered those who remained the terms given to a garrison that surrenders, and the Spanish accepted. When he rode up to receive the surrender, some Spanish soldiers fired, perhaps thinking a charge was coming. The French attacked again, giving no quarter, and the Spanish army nearly disappeared."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "French losses were about 4,000 men, killed and wounded. Spanish losses are more disputed. Two days later, Melo reported to Madrid 6,000 casualties and 4,000 prisoners. The English Wikipedia gives 7,300 to 15,000 killed, wounded and captured, depending on the author. Of the roughly 7,000 soldiers of the Spanish infantry, only 390 officers and 1,386 soldiers returned to Flanders, according to González de León. The Spanish lost all their cannon.",
        "The French lifted the siege of Rocroi, but could not advance on Flanders: the Spanish reorganized quickly. Enghien needed a month to reorganize his army and took Thionville on 8 August. Melo wrote that it was the most considerable defeat those provinces had ever suffered.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "For France, the victory strengthened the regency of Anne of Austria and Mazarin's position, and made the Duke of Enghien a hero. For Spain, it was the end of the invincible reputation of its tercios. The war between the two only ended in 1659, with the Peace of the Pyrenees, after the French won again at the Dunes in 1658.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The 'end of the tercios': for centuries it was said that Rocroi finished off the Spanish infantry. More recent historians disagree. The tercios won other battles afterward, such as Valenciennes (1656), and many place the turning point at the Dunes, in 1658. Nor was Rocroi decisive for the war: the Spanish had won at Honnecourt (1642), and the Imperials at Tuttlingen (1643). Some Spanish sources say only three of the five tercios were destroyed and that the other two left the field with their colors and weapons." },
        { note: "The surrender and the massacre: the most common account is the one above, with shots fired just as Enghien approached. There is no way to know whether it was a misunderstanding or a betrayal, and the French and Spanish versions differ." },
        { note: "Fontaines's chair was taken by the French as a trophy and is now in the Army Museum at Les Invalides, in Paris. The museum says Enghien is supposed to have said that, had he not won that day, he would have liked to die as Fontaines did." },
        { note: "Condé's turnabout: during the Fronde, in 1653, he went over to the Spanish side and commanded Spanish troops who retook Rocroi. The Peace of the Pyrenees returned the town to France." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  shanhaiguan: {
    war: "Fall of the Ming dynasty and rise of the Qing (1644)",
    hook: "A general opened the gate of the Great Wall to the age-old enemy, and China got a new dynasty.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1644, the [[Ming dynasty|ming]] was broken. Famine, plague, lack of money and peasant revolts had consumed the empire, and in the north, on the other side of the Great Wall, there was a well-organized enemy: the Manchus.",
        "The Manchus descended from the Jurchens and had built a military state under Nurhaci and his son Hong Taiji, who in 1636 gave it the name Great Qing. Their army was organized into the [[Eight Banners|oito bandeiras]], and the Manchus had been taking cities of Liaodong from the Ming, one by one. In September 1643, Hong Taiji died. A committee of princes chose his five-year-old son as heir, with Dorgon and Jirgalang as [[regents|regente]]. Dorgon soon became the strongman.",
        { h: "The road to the pass" },
        { timeline: [
          ["February 1644", "The rebel Li Zicheng founds the Shun dynasty."],
          ["5 April", "Emperor Chongzhen asks the frontier troops for help."],
          ["24 April", "Li breaks through the walls of Beijing."],
          ["25 April", "The emperor dies on Jingshan hill, behind the palace, where he hanged himself."],
          ["5 and 10 May", "Wu Sangui, back at Shanhai Pass, repels two Shun attacks."],
          ["18 May", "Li Zicheng goes in person, with about 60,000 men, to attack Wu."],
          ["25 May", "Li takes position west of the pass. Dorgon receives Wu's letter and sets out on a forced march."],
          ["27 May", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "General Wu Sangui commanded the Ming army guarding the Great Wall. He was marching toward Beijing to defend the emperor when he learned that the city had fallen. He went back to Shanhai Pass, the point where the Wall meets the sea. Now he was between two enemies: Li Zicheng on one side and the Manchus on the other."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Wu Sangui" },
        "A Ming general, he commanded the frontier army. Historians argue about his motives. What the sources record is that his father, Wu Xiang, was in Beijing, in Li Zicheng's hands, and that Li killed him. Between the rebel and the Manchus, Wu chose the Manchus.",
        { h: "Dorgon" },
        "Regent of the Qing and half-brother of the late Hong Taiji. His advisers urged him to take the [[Mandate of Heaven|mandato do céu]], and a Ming general's request for help gave him the chance. According to the Chinese Wikipedia, he demanded that Wu shave his head in the Manchu fashion and promised him a bride from the imperial family for his son.",
        { h: "Li Zicheng" },
        "A peasant rebel who had overthrown the Ming in Beijing and proclaimed himself emperor. He had plenty of men, but little experience of the terrain in that region."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are much debated, and the old sources exaggerate. The best modern ranges, according to the English Wikipedia:",
        { timeline: [
          ["Li Zicheng", "60,000 (Wakeman) to more than 100,000 (Mote). Early Qing sources go as high as 200,000, which Wakeman considers inflated."],
          ["Wu Sangui", "About 50,000 regular soldiers and 50,000 militiamen (Wakeman); 80,000 plus 20,000 to 30,000 militiamen (Mote); 40,000 plus 70,000 Liaodong residents (Hsi)."],
          ["Qing", "60,000 (Cao); the Chinese Wikipedia speaks of 70,000 to 80,000, among Manchus, Mongols and Han Chinese."]
        ]},
        { note: "The dates: Chinese sources give the days in the lunar calendar (the Chinese Wikipedia speaks of the 21st to 23rd days of the fourth month), and English ones convert them to 27 May 1644. The sources also disagree on whether Wu knelt before Dorgon on the eve or on the morning of the battle." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Shanhai Pass lies where the Great Wall meets the Bohai Sea, and it was the door between Manchuria and the Beijing plain. Li took position west of the pass, near the Sha River. According to Wakeman, there was a sandstorm in the afternoon, and the Chinese Wikipedia says the wind raised dust and hampered Li, who did not know the terrain.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "On 25 May, Dorgon received Wu's formal letter and set out on a forced march. According to the English Wikipedia, he covered some 150 km in about 24 hours, and on the way destroyed the few men of Tang Tong, a Ming officer who had surrendered to Li. In the morning, on reaching the pass, Dorgon accepted Wu's formal surrender. Wu's soldiers tied white cloths on their bodies so they could be recognized among the Shun.",
        "The first charge was Wu's. His vanguard attacked, failed to break Li's line and took heavy casualties, and for much of the day Wu fought almost alone. Dorgon waited, and the Qing only entered the fight late in the afternoon.",
        "In the afternoon, the wind and sand rose. According to Wakeman, Dorgon's cavalry, commanded by Ajige and Dodo, attacked Li's left wing at Yipianshi, and the Shun line broke. The Chinese Wikipedia adds that, around 3 p.m., Li, watching from a hill, was told by a monk that the white-banner cavalry was not Wu's, and he fled. Thousands of Shun soldiers died in the retreat toward Yongping."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "It is not known how many died: the sources say 'tens of thousands' on the Shun side, and many of them were forced laborers, not soldiers. Wu Sangui was promoted to Prince of Pingxi on 28 May and went to pursue Li.",
        "Li returned to Beijing on 31 May, declared himself emperor on 3 June and, the next day, burned the palaces and fled. Dorgon entered Beijing on 5 June, to the surprise of the population, which had expected Wu to restore the Ming. The young Shunzhi Emperor arrived on 19 October and was crowned on 8 November 1644. Li Zicheng died in September 1645, by suicide or killed by a peasant militia.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The battle opened the gates of China to the Manchus. They ruled until 1912, 268 years, as the Qing dynasty. Resistance was fierce: the order to shave the head in the Manchu style, of July 1645, set off revolts. And Wu Sangui himself, after helping to finish off the southern Ming, rebelled against the Qing in the Revolt of the Three Feudatories (1673–1681).",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The numbers: the ranges for Li Zicheng run from 60,000 to 400,000, according to the Chinese text. Wakeman considers the figures of the early Qing sources inflated." },
        { note: "Chen Yuanyuan: according to a popular tradition, Wu Sangui acted because of a concubine, Chen Yuanyuan, seized by the rebels. It is a theme of poems and novels, very well known in China, but historians treat it as legend. What is in the sources is the death of his father and other relatives." },
        { note: "Wu Sangui is remembered to this day as the man who opened the gate. What the sources show, however, is a negotiation in which Wu asked Dorgon for help, not a gate opened in secret." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  guararapes: {
    war: "Pernambucan Insurrection and the war against Dutch Brazil (1645–1654)",
    hook: "On two flooded hills near Recife, 2,200 men faced more than twice as many Dutch soldiers.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1630, the [[West India Company|wic]] took Olinda and Recife and came to control Brazil's Northeast, where the richest sugar mills in the world stood. The most famous governor was Maurice of Nassau, who stayed from 1637 to 1644. Portugal, for its part, had just separated from Spain, in 1640.",
        "After Nassau went back to Europe, the Luso-Brazilian landowners, in debt to the Company, rose up. The Pernambucan Insurrection began in 1645, led by the sugar-mill owner João Fernandes Vieira, and the Dutch were bottled up in Recife.",
        { h: "The road to Guararapes" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1630", "The Dutch take Olinda and Recife."],
          ["1645", "The insurrection begins."],
          ["1647", "The Company sends reinforcements: according to Wikipedia, 41 ships and about 6,000 men."],
          ["18 April 1648", "The Dutch leave Recife heading south."],
          ["19 April 1648", "First battle."],
          ["19 February 1649", "Second battle, in the same place."],
          ["1654", "The Dutch surrender in Recife and leave Brazil."]
        ]},
        "In April 1648, the Dutch decided to put an end to the resistance. Their commander, Sigismund von Schkoppe, wanted to take Muribeca, on the road to Cabo de Santo Agostinho, to cut the Luso-Brazilians off from their supplies and then destroy them."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Francisco Barreto de Meneses" },
        "Commander of the Luso-Brazilian forces. Instead of waiting for the attack, he chose to meet the Dutch on the road, even though he had about half their number and no artillery (according to the English Wikipedia; other sources mention six cannon).",
        { h: "The other commanders" },
        "João Fernandes Vieira commanded the tercio in the center. Filipe Camarão, an Indigenous chief, and Henrique Dias, leader of a tercio of Black soldiers, held the flanks. André Vidal de Negreiros had the fifth tercio, in reserve. Antônio Dias Cardoso commanded the scouts. Camarão died shortly after the first battle, in August 1648 (24 August, according to the English Wikipedia, though sources differ), and so did not fight in the second.",
        { h: "On the Dutch side" },
        "Sigismund von Schkoppe commanded in 1648 and left wounded. In 1649, the commander was Colonel Van den Brinck, who died in the fight."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Luso-Brazilians had about 2,200 men. The Dutch had 4,500 to 5,000, according to most sources; one Brazilian source (Toda Matéria) says 7,400. According to David Marley, in 1649 the Dutch were mostly European mercenaries, many of them German, with low morale and with clothes and armor too heavy for the heat.",
        { note: "The numbers for Guararapes vary a good deal, and the Dutch losses even more. In the sources we consulted there are three versions: about 1,000 casualties, with 515 dead (Estratégia Militares); 1,200 dead and 700 wounded (Toda Matéria); and 'about 2,000 dead' for the two battles together (A Gazeta). We use ranges." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Guararapes hill is in Jaboatão dos Guararapes, near Recife. The road linking Recife to the south went through the Boqueirão, a narrow pass between the hills and the mangrove swamp. Flooded ground on both sides forced the Dutch to march in a narrow column, which canceled the advantage of their wide lines.",
        { h: "The first battle, 19 April 1648" },
        "Barreto occupied the hills at night, before the Dutch. He drew up five [[tercios|terço]]: Vieira's in the center, Camarão and Dias on the flanks and Vidal de Negreiros in reserve. Dias Cardoso's scouts made first contact and drew the Dutch vanguard into the Boqueirão, pretending to fight against the main body of the resistance.",
        "Schkoppe launched three battalions against Vieira and Camarão and two others to flank the Luso-Brazilians, which Henrique Dias held off. Two other battalions stayed in reserve and never even fought. In the tight terrain, gunfire was of little use, and the fight was with swords and hand-to-hand weapons. Those who fled on the Dutch left got stuck in the swamp; those who fled on the right pushed Henrique Dias's men back toward Recife. Schkoppe was wounded and withdrew.",
        { h: "The second battle, 19 February 1649" },
        "The Dutch left Recife with about 3,500 to more than 5,000 men, depending on the source, and expected the Luso-Brazilians to use the road. But these came out of the swamp and the hills from the sides, used smaller trails, fired from inside the brush and then attacked at close range. After a few hours, the Dutch fell back to Recife and left their artillery behind."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "In the first battle, the Luso-Brazilians lost around 80 dead and 400 wounded, and the Dutch about a thousand men, killed, wounded or captured (the number changes from source to source). In the second, the Luso-Brazilians lost 45 to 47 dead and about 200 wounded. The Dutch lost 1,045 men (according to the English Wikipedia) or about 2,000 (according to other sources), and Van den Brinck was mortally wounded.",
        "The Dutch were locked up in Recife. With the loss of Angola, which the Portuguese retook in 1648 and which supplied the enslaved people for the sugar mills, the Company concluded that Brazil was no longer worth the effort. In January 1654, the Dutch surrendered at Campina do Taborda. In 1661, a treaty signed in The Hague confirmed the end of the dispute.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Guararapes is remembered in Brazil as the moment when white, Indigenous and Black people fought together against an invader. In 1656, Barreto had the church of Nossa Senhora dos Prazeres built on the site, listed as heritage in 1961. In the 19th century, Victor Meirelles painted the battle in a large canvas (1875–1879), which helped to fix this image in the country's memory.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Army Day: since 1994, 19 April has been Brazilian Army Day, by decision of Minister Zenildo, according to the sociologist Celso Castro. It was an institutional choice, not a traditional date." },
        { note: "'Cradle of the Army': historians such as Marcelo Cheche Galves and Paulo Henrique Martinez recall that in 1648 there was no Brazil and no Brazilian Army, only a Portuguese colony. The idea that the battle united the 'three races' was built by nationalist writers in the 19th century. Castro observes that the fight was carried out almost entirely by local people, with little help from Portugal." },
        { note: "The names: the spelling of the Dutch commander of 1648 varies in the sources (Schkoppe, Schoppe). The one of 1649 appears as Van den Brinck and also as Van den Brinken." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  ambuila: {
    war: "War between Portugal and the Kingdom of Kongo (17th century)",
    hook: "On a day in October 1665, the Kingdom of Kongo lost its king, its crown and a good part of its nobility.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Kingdom of Kongo had been Christian since the end of the 15th century, exchanged ambassadors with Lisbon and Rome and had been, in the 16th century, a trading partner of Portugal. That changed after the Portuguese founded Luanda in 1575 and the colony of Angola grew up beside it.",
        { h: "The road to Ambuila" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1575", "Portugal founds Luanda."],
          ["1622", "The Portuguese invade southern Kongo and win at Mbumbi."],
          ["1641", "Kongo allies with the Dutch, who take Luanda."],
          ["1648", "The Portuguese expel the Dutch from Angola."],
          ["22 December 1663", "The king of Portugal orders Governor André Vidal de Negreiros to take Kongo's copper mines."],
          ["29 October 1665", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The governors of Angola began to attack a buffer region between the two kingdoms, the Dembos, which both claimed as theirs. The king of Kongo, António I, tried to win Spain's support against Portugal, without success, and looked for allies among the Dembos and in Mbwila (Ambuila). In 1665, a succession quarrel in Mbwila made both sides send armies: the king of Mbwila, backed by Kongo, against his aunt, backed by Portugal. As for the mines, António answered that they did not exist and that he owed nothing to the king of Portugal."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "António I, king of Kongo" },
        "He was the [[manikongo|manicongo]]. Born in 1617, he had ruled since 1661, when he was elected after the death of Garcia II. He saw the Portuguese pressure on the southern border and tried to secure himself with allies. When war came, he went at the head of the army, with a guard of about 400 swordsmen.",
        { h: "Luís Lopes de Sequeira" },
        "Captain-general of the Portuguese forces in Angola, of Portuguese and Angolan descent. He commanded few European soldiers alongside large African contingents. He died in 1681, murdered by a Portuguese soldier at the battle of Katole.",
        { h: "The Duke of Bengo" },
        "He commanded Kongo's reserve. It was he who covered the army's flight, and that is why there were survivors."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Portuguese had about 450 musketeers and two pieces of light artillery, plus large allied African forces, including [[imbangala|imbangala]], held in reserve. The Portuguese Wikipedia says the total reached some 15,000 men, including soldiers from Brazil. Kongo had many archers (the Portuguese Wikipedia says 15,000), about 5,000 heavy infantry, with shields and swords, and a regiment of 380 musketeers.",
        { note: "The numbers for Ambuila are very uncertain. Even the English Wikipedia contradicts itself between the text and the infobox, which gives 21,900 to 29,000 Kongolese and 14,150 to 14,500 Portuguese. We use the numbers in the text, based on the historian John Thornton, and make clear that they are estimates." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The two armies marched for several days and met in the valley of the Ulanga River, south of Ambuila. Steep mountains and the river closed off the east side of the field, and there were low hills to the west.",
        { h: "The battle in four movements" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Portuguese occupied the space between the river and the hills. They drew up the musketeers in a diamond formation, in the center, with the artillery in front, and put the African allies on the flanks. Kongo advanced with a vanguard, three divisions of heavy infantry and archers on the flanks.",
        "Second, the Kongolese archers drove off most of the Portuguese side's African archers. The allies left the field, and the musketeers were left without their flanks.",
        "Third, Kongo's heavy infantry and musketeers attacked the Portuguese musketeers, but could not break the formation. The Portuguese musketeers held.",
        "Fourth, in the last attack, King António was killed. With the king's fall, almost the whole Kongolese army fell apart. Only the reserve of the Duke of Bengo kept order and let the survivors escape."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to the English Wikipedia, some 5,000 Kongolese were killed or captured. Among the dead were the king, two of his sons (according to the infobox), two nephews, four governors, 95 titleholders and some 400 nobles. The king's chaplain, the Capuchin friar Francisco de São Salvador, also died. A seven-year-old son of the king was captured. Portuguese losses were not recorded.",
        "The king's head was buried with honors in the chapel of Nossa Senhora de Nazaré, overlooking the bay of Luanda. The crown and scepter were sent to Lisbon as trophies.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "With no clear heir and many candidates dead or captured, Kongo plunged into a decades-long civil war between the rival houses Kimpanzu and Kinlaza. The kingdom did not disappear, but it stopped functioning as a single state. Portugal, for its part, could not keep control and only returned in 1693. For many Congolese historians, Ambuila is the turning point in the kingdom's history.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: the battle appears as Ambuila, Mbwila, Ambuíla and Mbuila, depending on the language and the source." },
        { note: "The captives: many of those captured were sold as enslaved people, and some were taken to Brazil. A tradition tied to the Quilombo of Palmares says that a woman named Aqualtune, daughter of a king of Kongo, commanded part of the warriors at Mbwila, was captured and taken to Brazil. She would be the mother of Ganga Zumba and the grandmother of Zumbi, the last great leader of Palmares. The 17th-century documents only mention her at Palmares, as the king's mother; her Kongolese origin and her presence at Mbwila do not appear in the chronicles of the time, and some consider her Zumbi's great-aunt. She is a partly legendary figure." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  saraighat: {
    war: "Ahom-Mughal wars (1615–1682)",
    hook: "A sick general had himself rowed to the front of the retreating fleet, and the battle turned.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Assam, in northeastern India, is the valley of the Brahmaputra River, closed in by mountains and forests. Since the 13th century it had been ruled by the Ahoms, and the [[Mughal Empire|mogol]], master of almost all of India, could not subdue it for good.",
        "In 1662, the Mughal general Mir Jumla invaded Assam and left the kingdom devastated and broke. The Treaty of Ghilajharighat, in January 1663, imposed an indemnity, the status of [[vassal|vassalo]] and the loss of territories. It was under this agreement that Ramani Gabharu, Lachit's niece, was handed over to the Mughals.",
        { h: "The road to Saraighat" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1662–1663", "Mir Jumla invades Assam. Treaty of Ghilajharighat."],
          ["1667", "The Ahoms retake Guwahati from the Mughals."],
          ["February 1669", "Emperor Aurangzeb sends Ram Singh I with a larger army. He reaches Rangamati."],
          ["Around August 1669", "At Alaboi, the Mughals defeat the Ahoms. Accounts speak of some 10,000 Ahom dead."],
          ["April 1670", "King Chakradhwaj Singha dies. His successor, Udayaditya, renews the war."],
          ["March 1671", "Battle of Saraighat."]
        ]},
        "After Alaboi, Ram Singh offered to return to the situation of 1639 in exchange for 300,000 rupees. The Ahoms refused."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Lachit Borphukan, 48 years old" },
        "Born in 1622, the son of a high official who had started out as a commoner, he was [[borphukan|borphukan]], viceroy of the west, and commander of the army. He retook Guwahati in 1667. According to Assamese tradition, he had his own uncle put to death for neglecting the construction of a defensive wall, with the line that his uncle was not greater than his country. No document of the time records this: what is recorded is that he ordered the death penalty for negligence.",
        { h: "Ram Singh I" },
        "Raja of Amber (Jaipur), a [[Rajput|rajputs]] in the service of Emperor Aurangzeb, sent with a far larger army to retake Guwahati. One account says that, even when defeated, he praised the quality of the Ahom soldiers.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Mughal side, Munnawar Khan was the admiral. On the Ahom side, King Udayaditya Singha, who came to the throne in 1670, sent about 20,000 men to Saraighat."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are uncertain. According to popular accounts, the Mughal army had 30,000 infantry and 18,000 cavalry, besides the contingents of 21 Rajput chiefs and archers from Koch Bihar, and a fleet with large boats, some with as many as 16 cannon. The Ahoms could in theory mobilize about 100,000 militiamen, but those at Saraighat were far fewer, and their strength was the fleet of small boats, built in their own shipyards.",
        { note: "The sources do not agree on the numbers for Saraighat, and many are popular rather than scholarly. The English Wikipedia gives different numbers in the text and in the infobox. We use only what is consensus: the Mughals were far more numerous." },
        { h: "The terrain and the plan" },
        "Guwahati lies between hills and forests, and the Ahoms raised earthen embankments around the city, so the Mughal cavalry had little room to run. This pushed the invaders toward the river, their weak point. The Ahom fort of Itakhuli stood on the south bank. At Saraighat, the Brahmaputra narrows to about a kilometer wide, and the lighter Ahom boats maneuvered better in that space than the heavy Mughal boats.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "According to the accounts, the Mughals advanced with the fleet toward Andharubali, and the Ahom boats began to fall back. Lachit, sick, was at Itakhuli. He ordered that he be taken onto the water and went to the front with a handful of boats (accounts differ on how many). Seeing him, the fleeing boats turned back, and others joined him, until the fleet met the Mughals in front of the Kamakhya hills.",
        "The Ahoms attacked from front and rear, with boats tied side by side to form a bridge, and Munnawar Khan was shot dead. The Mughals fell back to the Manas River, and Lachit forbade pursuit. Another version says it was the adviser Achyutanda Doloi who persuaded him to stop."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Munnawar Khan, three other high-ranking Mughal commanders and some 4,000 men died, according to the English Wikipedia. There is no figure for Ahom casualties. Ram Singh left Kamrup on 7 April 1671 (the Wikipedia article on the Ahom-Mughal wars says he fell back to Rangamati in March).",
        "Lachit died in April 1672, aged 49, at Kaliabor, of an illness the sources do not name. In 1679, the borphukan Laluk Sola defected and handed Guwahati over to the Mughals, but in 1682 the Ahoms retook it, and Mughal control in Kamrup ended.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Assam was not conquered by the Mughals. In parts of Assam, 24 November, Lachit's birthday, is remembered as Lachit Divas. The bridge over the Brahmaputra at Guwahati has been called Saraighat since 1962. And the best cadet of India's National Defence Academy receives the Lachit Borphukan gold medal.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The legends: some famous stories about Lachit, such as that he answered Ram Singh that 'lions fight at night' or that Munnawar Khan was smoking a hookah when he was shot, come from chronicles or popular tradition, not from documents that can be checked." },
        { note: "The date: most sources say March 1671, but one popular source says 5 April." },
        { note: "The classic biography, by S. K. Bhuyan (1947), gave the legend of Lachit academic standing. That is why it is worth separating what the chronicles and documents say from what popular memory added later." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  lund: {
    war: "Scanian War (1675–1679)",
    hook: "On a winter day, a 21-year-old king nearly lost the battle by chasing the enemy too far, and won it by coming back.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Scania is the far south of Sweden, a few kilometers from Denmark. It was Danish for centuries and passed to Sweden in the Treaty of Roskilde, in 1658. The Danes never accepted it.",
        "The chance came in 1675. Sweden, which had entered the war on the side of France, lost at Fehrbellin and saw Denmark win at sea. In June 1676, the Danes landed at Helsingborg with about 14,000 men and had the support of the local peasants. Within a month, only Malmö was still Swedish.",
        { h: "The road to Lund" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1658", "The Treaty of Roskilde gives Scania to Sweden."],
          ["June 1676", "The Danes land at Helsingborg."],
          ["August 1676", "The Swedes win at Halmstad."],
          ["Autumn 1676", "About 14,000 Swedes gather and march south to relieve Malmö, but disease, cold and hunger weaken them. The camp at Bårslöv became famous for dysentery."],
          ["November 1676", "The Danes hold Lund and the crossings of the Kävlingeån river. Snow and ice make the crossing possible."],
          ["4 December 1676", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The Swedes had once numbered some 14,000 men, three quarters of them mounted, but on the day of the battle they were about 8,000. The Danes, thinking the enemy had gone into winter quarters, were caught by surprise."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Protagonists", blocks: [
        { h: "Charles XI of Sweden, 21 years old" },
        "King since childhood. He personally commanded the right wing and made a courageous attack. He was criticized for getting carried away and chasing the enemy for so long, leaving the rest of the army in trouble. But he came back in time, and the victory made him a popular king.",
        { h: "Simon Grundel-Helmfelt" },
        "A Swedish field marshal who commanded the army alongside the king. It was he who ordered the slaughter stopped when the battle was over.",
        { h: "Christian V of Denmark" },
        "The Danish king, who commanded the army with General Carl von Arensdorff (wounded early in the battle) and who saw his army fall apart.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Swedish side, Johan Galle commanded the left and died; Otto Wilhelm von Fersen, of the vanguard, was gravely wounded; Nils Bielke held his squadrons together in the right-hand cavalry. On the Danish side, the general's brother, Friedrich von Arensdorff, took command when Carl fell."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Swedes had about 8,000 men (2,000 on foot, 6,000 mounted) and 12 cannon. The Danes had about 13,000 (6,300 on foot, 6,000 mounted) and 51 to 56 cannon. Denmark had more men and far more artillery, but the Swedes had fewer mercenaries, and the Danish cavalry still used the [[caracole|caracol]], which made it slow. There were also a few hundred Dutch sailors alongside the Danes.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The fields north of Lund, with low hills, walls and ditches, and the frozen Kävlingeån river at the Swedes' backs. The battle front ran for roughly a kilometer, north to south, with the Danes to the east.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "In the early hours of 4 December, with no Moon, the Swedes crossed the ice of the Kävlingeån in several columns and reached Stångby around 5:30. The plan was to attack the sleeping Danish camp with cavalry. But the scouts saw that the ground was no good for horses, and a council of war advised against an infantry attack. The king agreed to occupy the hills north of Lund.",
        { h: "The battle in five beats" },
        "First, the Danes woke up and ran to the same hills. The clash between the Swedish right and the Danish left ended in a draw, but the Swedes kept the heights and pushed the Danes east. In this phase, Otto Wilhelm von Fersen and Carl von Arensdorff were wounded.",
        "Second, around 9 a.m., the main battle began. Charles XI led the attack on the Danish left flank. There was hard cavalry fighting among walls and ditches. Around 10:30, the Danish left wing gave way. The Swedish right, with the king, passed through the Danish camp at Skälshög and pursued those fleeing toward the river, for about 8 km. Many Danes went onto the ice, which broke, and drowned.",
        "Third, while the king was away, the Danish right pushed the Swedish center and left back. The Swedish commander of the left, Johan Galle, was killed, and the Swedes were in trouble, with their backs to the town wall. Friedrich von Arensdorff, who had taken over the Danish command, had the enemy at his mercy. Instead of attacking, he ordered the troops reorganized, around noon, and the fighting stopped.",
        "Fourth, Charles XI came back around 3 p.m. with the cavalry, attacked the Danes from the rear and was joined by other troops who broke through the lines. In about half an hour, the Danish army fell apart.",
        "Fifth, the slaughter. Many Danish infantry soldiers were killed, until Helmfelt ordered it stopped. The ceasefire signal sounded at 5 p.m. Of the roughly 1,300 Dutch sailors, only a few dozen survived."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The sources differ. According to the English Wikipedia, the Swedes lost 1,000 to 1,500 dead and 2,000 to 2,500 wounded. The Danes lost 2,000 to 2,500 dead, 4,000 to 4,500 wounded and about 2,000 prisoners (the Swedish Wikipedia speaks of 1,500 to 2,000 prisoners and 51 cannon lost). Swedish counts of the time, of 8,300 to 9,000 dead, are considered inflated, and current estimates lie between 3,000 and 4,000 dead. According to Danish sources, Denmark's army was left with about 5,000 men fit to fight.",
        "The remains of the Danish army withdrew to Landskrona. Eight months later, reinforced by Austrian and German troops, the Danes met the Swedes again, at the battle of Landskrona.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In proportion to the number of combatants, Lund is one of the bloodiest battles in Scandinavian history, and Scania remains Swedish. The victory is attributed to a better mix of cavalry and infantry, to fewer mercenaries and to the ability to counterattack quickly. But the battle did not end the war, which only finished in 1679.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The calendar: Sweden used the Julian calendar, so 4 December 1676 is the 14th in the calendar we use today. Some sources write '4/14 December'." },
        { note: "Charles XI's role: the king was criticized for chasing the enemy and nearly letting the army lose. It was his return, and not the pursuit, that decided the day. It is a classic example of how success at one end of the line can be dangerous." },
        { note: "The death toll: the difference between the old counts (8,000 to 9,000) and the current ones (3,000 to 4,000) may come from the old calculations including those who died later, in the camps, of wounds and disease." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

